do you ever imagine your own funeral I  do in fact it may be something I think  about a little bit too much given my age  and health and happiness but as someone  with an interest in liturgy and an  inclination towards self-reflection  imagining and even planning my own  funeral is a fascinating possibility  fairs that I Britain be used let there  be liturgical dance but photos will  surface who will attend who will speak  and most importantly what will they say  I hope they will say that I loved they  may speak about my academic not  irritable or resentful it does not  rejoice in wrongdoing but rejoices in  the truth love bears all things believes  all things hopes all things endures all  things love never ends ah we could bask  in the beauty of Paul's ode to love all  day this is an incredibly evocative  description of love and for many of us  it brings to mind romantic love this  passage is often read at weddings and  with februari 14th only two weeks away  we are surrounded by eternal devotion  declared on candy hearts shop windows  full of pink prepackaged romance and  invitations to speed dating at gypsy  but Paul is writing about more than  romantic love he is writing about the  love of a whole community a community  composed of people very different from  one another poor and rich female and  male liberal and conservative  politically socially and theologically  and Paul is exhorting them all to love  one another when we listen to this  description of love with this context in  mind we realize how difficult and  demanding real love is love is patient  love is kind love is not envious  boastful arrogant or rude love does not  insist on its own way love is not  irritable or resentful love does not  rejoice in wrongdoing but rejoices in  the truth love bears all things believes  all things hopes all things endures all  things love never ends is there no  escape suddenly love has become a lot  more challenging suddenly love is not a  warm fuzzy feeling or even a deep  abiding commitment suddenly love is  about everything we do everything we  think everything we feel our attitudes  our habits our actions our beliefs our  hopes love is demanded in every aspect  of our lives and love is not only about  one romantic partner but about everyone  friends family enemies the people we  don't even know exist this radical love  described by Paul is mysterious we can  only know it in part we can only see it  through a mirror dimly we can only  glimpse shadowy reflections of this love  but these shadowy reflections are the  most important thing in the world we can  glimpse this love by looking at the  people in our lives and in our faith  traditions who embody love we can  glimpse this love by telling the real  love stories  my former pastor and colleague Joelle  showed me what it means to love in the  most mundane and ordinary moments Joelle  took time with each person he  encountered to make that person feel  like they mattered the custodian at  church the photographer at the reception  the other passenger in the elevator the  quiet youth on the fringes of the church  youth group and the rebel who everyone  else had already given up on sure well  was often late and I will admit that  people who are late normally really  annoy me but was so well it didn't  bother me quite as much because I  suspected that he was late because he  had stopped to listen to someone who  needed to talk or taken that extra  moment to acknowledge that a service  worker was a person not a machine love  is patient love is kind love takes time  for people no matter what who has shown  you love that is patient and kind Jesus  returned to his hometown stood up in  front of the religious community that  raised him and declared i am the  fulfillment of scripture I am the  Messiah we heard about this in the  glorious gospel reading last week and  this week we heard the results members  of Jesus community ran him out of town  and threatened to throw him off a cliff  this is the good news of the gospel love  is about speaking the truth even when  it's risky love is about saying the  things that you just aren't supposed to  say love is not always about being nice  love rejoices in the truth even the hard  truth no matter what who has given you a  glimpse of love that rejoices in the  truth  this community at university church has  also taught me about love you are some  of the most talented and impressive  people I have ever met I tremble at the  thought of your resumes and soon many of  you will have a label permanently  attached to your name that will declare  to the world you are exceptional at the  same time you have shown me what it  means to love in ways that are not  boastful or arrogant or rude some of you  have shown love through the questions  you asked and your readiness to learn  some of you have shown love through your  openness to trying new things even  things you might not be good at some of  you have shown love through your  willingness to help out with no  expectation of recognition or reward  love is not boastful or arrogant or rude  love is humble no matter what you have  accomplished who has given you a glimpse  of humble love  my grandma and grandpa were married in  1942 I remember the huge party  celebrating their 50th wedding  anniversary in 1992 I had a brand-new  pink dress and helped decorate their  cars with colorful flowers and streamers  over the following 10 years my  grandfather's health began to fail my  grandparents moved out of their house  and into a condo eventually my  grandfather moved into the nursing home  next door my grandfather my grandmother  walked over every day to visit him when  my grandparents celebrated their 61st  wedding anniversary in 2003 both my  grandmother and grandfather were very  ill the night before my grandmother died  she had a lucid moment and a nurse  recorded her words she spoke of her love  for her children and grandchildren and  of her love for her husband even though  he could no longer read the newspaper at  first I found these last words tragic my  grandmother's final thoughts of my  grandfather were about how he was no  longer the man he used to be now I hear  it differently my grandmother's last  words were about how much she loved my  grandfather despite and because he was  no longer the man she had married 61  years before within a week of my  grandmother's death my grandfather also  passed away after more than six decades  how could they be apart love endures  love survives challenge and change no  matter what who has given you a glimpse  of love without indoors  Dirk Willems was one of my forebearers  in faith in the Mennonite faith  tradition he was a 16th century church  leader in the Netherlands who was  imprisoned on account of his beliefs  after being in prison for a year Dirk  escaped he was pursued across a frozen  river by a prison guard and after he had  safely reached the far bank he heard a  loud crack behind him the ice had broken  and the guard had fallen through into  the frigid water Dirk hesitated for only  a second before turning around inching  across the ice and extending his hand to  his captor he pulled the guard safely to  shore and the guard wanted to let dirt  go but his supervisor arrived and called  out to him to halt Dirk the guard  returned Dirk to prison and he was  burned at the stake several months later  Dirk Williams died for his faith a faith  that taught him that the lives of his  enemies were worth saving even at the  cost of his own life love does not  insist on its own way love puts the  other person first no matter what who  has given you a glimpse of love but does  not insist on its own way  after hearing these stories how can I  even hope to embody a fraction of the  love lived out by these individuals and  communities how can I presume to suggest  that at my funeral people would  celebrate that I loved I am overwhelmed  by my own failure to love and by a world  that so often seems so inhospitable to  love and then I remember that love is  not all about me sometimes it may feel  like it's all about me my feelings my  thoughts my desires my actions but love  is not all about me at other times it  feels like love is all about other  people about putting the feelings  thoughts desires and actions of others  first but love is not all about other  people either love is all about God love  is all about God being all about  everyone it is about God loving other  people in God loving me and especially  about God bringing you and me and us and  them together because love is all about  God being all about everyone love can  never be separated from justice or  forgiveness regardless of what I believe  in this moment about God and whether God  exists or as powerful or good love is  still about God love is something beyond  and within us something that moves us  towards one another something that moves  us towards our most honest selves this  kind of God this kind of love is  something to celebrate God's love  revealed in human love is something to  celebrate so we celebrate the love of  joelle and Jesus and university church  and my grandparents and Dirk and all of  the other people who have given us  glimpses of God's love we celebrate love  that is patient and kind love that  rejoices in the truth love that is  humble love that indoors love that does  not insist on its own way  love that bears all things believes all  things hopes all things love that never  ends we celebrate God's love reflected  in human love in every moment of life  love present when sperm meets egg when  lungs meet air when parents meet child  when child meets friends when youth  fleet enemies when people meet passions  when partner needs partner when age  meets wisdom when body needs earth when  spirit needs spirit love never ends this  is the type of love I hope friends and  family will celebrate at my funeral this  is the type of love I hope to celebrate  in every moment of my life this is the  type of love I hope our life together  can celebrate God is this love this is  God's love amen  you
 

